  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nShowers associated with a shortwave and elevated WAA will drift off \nto the east-southeast by ~19z. Another upstream low amplitude, \nconvectively augmented, shortwave follows east into the lower Great \nLakes late this afternoon into this evening with additional \nshowers expected, mainly along/north of US 24. \n\nMonday could very well be similar to today with clouds and showers \naround as moisture continues to stream in on the northern periphery \nof an upper level ridge centered over the Southern Plains and Mid MS \nRiver Valley. However, this heat dome will be gradually building \nnorthward which would orient the low level jet core and associated \nmoisture transport more off to the the north-northwest when compared \nto today. Opted to introduce some low-end PoPs far north to account \nfor the close proximity and latest model guidance. Ongoing southerly \nflow in the low levels (sfc dewpoints nearing 70F), in combination \nwith any filtered sunshine, may push afternoon heat indices well \ninto the 90s otherwise by Monday afternoon, best chances across the \nsouthern half of the forecast area. \n\nNear advisory level heat/humidity becomes the primary concern into \nTuesday and Wednesday as the impressive ~596 dam 5H ridge drifts \neast over the OH Valley. Higher confidence in peak PM heat indices \nnearing 100F continues to be along and south of the US 30 corridor \neach day. Northern portions may struggle with convective cloud \ndebris (thus slightly cooler) from what should be a periodically \nstormy pattern north of the area. The NBM continued with its dry \nforecast each day, but would not be surprised if at least a low PoP \nis introduced in the north at some point. \n\nModels continue to break down the upper ridging for the second half \nof the week which should bring slightly cooler temps and periodic \nchances (20-40%) for convection as several shortwaves and a frontal \nboundary drop through in developing northwest flow aloft.",
